<Article slug="memory-and-performance">
<p>A cached hash contains 512 short fields. Adding one more field sounds like a small change. In the Redis 8.2.2 lab for this article, its reported memory allocation rose from 7,200 bytes to 37,008 bytes.</p>
<p>The new field did not contain thirty kilobytes of text. The hash crossed a configured threshold and changed its internal representation. Toggle the extra field to compare the two measured states.</p>
<MemoryEncoding />
<p>The first state uses a <strong>listpack</strong>, a compact representation for a small aggregate. The second uses a hash table. The API is still HSET and HGET, but the layout behind it has changed. A sizing estimate based only on the new field’s text would miss most of the increase.</p>
<p>This is one reason “the data fits in memory” is not a complete capacity argument. Which representation stores it? How much memory does the process retain? What happens while the dataset changes, a large reply waits to be sent, or a background snapshot is running? We need to follow both the stored data and the work performed around it.</p>

<h2>MEMORY USAGE and internal encoding</h2>
<p><code>MEMORY USAGE key</code> estimates the allocations required for a key and its value, including associated administrative overhead. It is closer to the question “what does keeping this entry cost?” than the length of its JSON representation. For nested structures it samples elements by default; <code>SAMPLES 0</code> examines them all and can therefore do substantially more work.</p>
<p><code>OBJECT ENCODING key</code> exposes the representation selected for an object. The opening lab also reads the configured <code>hash-max-listpack-entries</code> and <code>hash-max-listpack-value</code> limits. They were 512 and 64 respectively. The example crosses the entry-count limit; a sufficiently large individual field or value can also force a change.</p>
<p>The same lab stores ordinary strings and reports the following observations. Values contain repeated ASCII characters, and key names differ as shown.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Value bytes</th><th>Key bytes</th><th>MEMORY USAGE</th><th>Encoding</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>0</td><td>7</td><td>32 bytes</td><td>embstr</td></tr>
<tr><td>16</td><td>8</td><td>48 bytes</td><td>embstr</td></tr>
<tr><td>1,024</td><td>10</td><td>1,312 bytes</td><td>raw</td></tr>
<tr><td>65,536</td><td>11</td><td>81,952 bytes</td><td>raw</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>These numbers belong to a 64-bit arm64 macOS build using libc, not to every Redis deployment. They show why measuring representative objects matters. A separate comparison keeps the value identical and increases its key name from one byte to one hundred; reported allocation rises from 48 to 144 bytes.</p>
<p>Small per-entry costs become substantial across millions of entries. Long repeated prefixes, many tiny objects, retained fields and serialisation choices all deserve measurement. Preserve the key’s correctness contract while improving its representation: removing a tenant or schema dimension to save bytes would recreate the collisions examined in <a href="/learn/databases/caching/keys-and-values/">cache keys and cached values</a>.</p>
<p>Compact encodings trade representation and lookup work against memory. Raising their thresholds can retain compact storage for larger objects, but also changes the workload Redis must perform. Measure reads, writes and conversions with the actual field counts and lengths before changing them. A memory saving on one synthetic object is not enough evidence for a configuration change.</p>
<p>Grouping entries into one aggregate can reduce repeated overhead, yet changes the unit retrieved, invalidated and distributed. A single enormous hash may save some metadata while creating a large response or a concentrated access point. Choose an object boundary that fits application access, not merely the representation with the smallest isolated allocation.</p>

<h2>maxmemory, resident memory and headroom</h2>
<p><code>maxmemory</code> governs Redis’s memory-pressure policy. It is not an operating-system limit on the process’s resident memory, nor a reservation of enough host memory for every operation. The distinction matters even if the cache is behaving exactly as configured.</p>
<p>The next model starts with more requested cache allocation than its eviction limit allows. It removes eligible entries until the counted amount fits. Then add memory for snapshot copies without changing the limit.</p>
<MemoryBudget />
<p>At the default settings, 1,024 MiB counts towards eviction and 376 MiB of requested cache allocation has been discarded. Add 1,024 MiB of snapshot copies: the model now needs 2,560 MiB against a 2,048 MiB host limit. The eviction accounting is still satisfied. A policy can be satisfied at one layer while capacity is exhausted at another.</p>
<p>The figure deliberately partitions its inputs so they can be added. Real Redis metrics overlap. In particular, do not add <code>used_memory</code> to <code>used_memory_rss</code>: they are different views of much of the same memory.</p>
<table class="cache-metrics"><thead><tr><th>Measurement</th><th>Question it helps answer</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><code>used_memory</code></td><td>How many bytes does Redis account as allocated through its allocator?</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>used_memory_dataset</code> and <code>used_memory_overhead</code></td><td>How is that allocation divided between dataset and reported overhead?</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>used_memory_rss</code></td><td>How much resident memory does the operating system report for the process?</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>mem_not_counted_for_evict</code></td><td>How much reported allocation is excluded from eviction accounting, such as relevant replication and AOF buffers?</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Allocator retention and process overhead can keep resident memory above the bytes currently allocated to objects. Deleting keys need not return the corresponding pages to the operating system immediately. A fragmentation ratio alone is also easy to misread: a large ratio on a small mostly empty process may represent few bytes. Compare the absolute difference, allocator-specific metrics and previous memory peak.</p>
<p>Size against the host or container limit that will actually be enforced. Include the operating system or other processes where relevant, client and replication activity, temporary work, and the configured persistence or recovery operations. Choose headroom from observed peaks and fault exercises rather than assuming a universal percentage.</p>
<p>Lowering maxmemory can recover room by admitting fewer cached objects, but may increase misses and source load. A capacity change is therefore also a workload change. Verify the effect on the source and on useful hit rate, using the working-set reasoning from <a href="/learn/databases/caching/expiration-and-eviction/">expiration and eviction</a>. A larger Redis allocation is not useful if it leaves the process unable to survive its normal maintenance.</p>

<h2>Copy-on-write during background operations</h2>
<p>Redis can fork a child process to produce a background snapshot. Initially, parent and child share memory pages. The child needs the old view while the parent continues serving changes. When the parent writes a shared page, the operating system preserves the old page and gives the parent a private copy.</p>
<p>Start a snapshot below. Write page 1 several times, then compare writing every page.</p>
<CopyOnWrite />
<p>Starting the model snapshot does not immediately double the twelve data pages. The first write to page 1 raises the physical total to thirteen. Further writes to that same private page leave the total at thirteen. Writing every page eventually raises it to twenty-four while the snapshot still needs all twelve old pages.</p>
<p>This is <strong>copy-on-write</strong>. Extra memory depends on which pages change while the child remains active, not just on the number of commands or the count of logical keys updated. A small update can touch a page containing other data. Actual allocation and freeing can create further effects omitted from this fixed-page model.</p>
<p>A background operation lasting longer gives the parent more time to modify shared pages. A write-heavy workload spread over the dataset can consequently need much more temporary physical memory than a quiet workload with the same stored keys. Redis’s copy-on-write measurements in <code>INFO persistence</code>, together with host observations, help establish that peak for the actual workload.</p>
<p>Fork itself has a cost, and the child’s storage work competes for resources too. “Background” means much of the work runs outside ordinary command execution; it does not mean no latency or memory effect. AOF rewriting also uses a forked process in this Redis baseline. We will examine persistence and recovery semantics in the next article; here the requirement is to leave room for the operations the deployment is configured to perform.</p>
<p>When the child exits, snapshot-only pages are no longer needed. That lifecycle event is distinct from how quickly memory statistics settle or an allocator returns pages. Do not use an immediate RSS drop as the only evidence that a background operation has finished.</p>

<h2>Large keys and expensive commands</h2>
<p>A cache hit avoids loading the source, but may still request a large amount of Redis work. HGET retrieves one hash field. HGETALL traverses and returns every field and value. In our native lab, the same hash produces one field for HGET and 513 fields for HGETALL. Whether the application needs all those fields is an application-design question.</p>
<p>There are several costs to follow: locating and traversing data, constructing a reply, transferring bytes, buffering them and decoding the result in the client. A command’s complexity describes part of that path, not the complete request latency. Large strings may require little key-lookup work while still producing large network and allocation costs.</p>
<p>Set limits on value bytes and aggregate cardinality, not just the number of keys. An unexpectedly large product, tenant or account can violate a budget that looked safe at the median. Decide whether the whole object is required, whether a smaller projection will do, and whether access can be divided into bounded pieces without breaking the consistency contract.</p>
<p>Compression can reduce storage and transfer bytes while adding CPU and application complexity. Partial reads may become harder when a whole encoded object must be decoded. Measure the operation that the user experiences, including the cost of creating and decoding the cache value, rather than comparing stored byte counts alone.</p>
<p>Review commands used by administration and diagnostics as carefully as those in request handling. KEYS examines the database keyspace in one command. A rare maintenance action can therefore disturb many otherwise small requests. Finding a few matching names does not mean the server only examined a few names.</p>

<h2>SCAN and incremental maintenance</h2>
<p>Breaking a long job into smaller Redis operations creates opportunities for other commands to run between them. It does not erase the total work. Compare one twelve-item operation with four smaller chunks in the model.</p>
<MaintenanceChunks />
<p>The first read waits ten milliseconds behind the long operation. With three-item chunks it waits one millisecond under the model’s explicit scheduling rule. Total execution work is unchanged. In a real system, additional commands and round trips add overhead, and scheduling is not the priority scheme shown here. The lesson is to create bounded opportunities for progress and verify them under contention.</p>
<p>The SCAN family provides cursor-based traversal. Start with cursor 0, then pass each returned cursor into the next call until Redis returns 0 again. An empty result batch is not the end. The lab’s scan for a nonexistent prefix receives only empty batches, yet must continue until its final cursor reaches 0.</p>
<p>COUNT is a hint about work, not a guaranteed result count or latency limit. Our compact 512-field hash returns all 512 fields from HSCAN even with COUNT 1. That concrete result is why code should not allocate a supposedly safe fixed-size result buffer based on COUNT.</p>
<p>A complete scan covers elements that remain present throughout it, but can return duplicates. Elements added or removed during traversal have weaker guarantees. It is not a snapshot or an ordered pagination API. Make repeated handling safe, and do not turn a background scan into an authoritative report of one instant without another consistency mechanism.</p>
<p>The work following each batch also needs bounds. A gentle scan followed by thousands of simultaneous GETs or deletions can still flood the client and server. Limit in-flight operations and bytes, pace maintenance, observe request latency, and stop or back off when its budget is exceeded.</p>

<h2>DEL, UNLINK and memory reclamation</h2>
<p>Removing a large aggregate has two distinct parts: making its key unavailable and reclaiming its internal allocations. DEL can perform substantial reclamation work on the command path. UNLINK separates key removal from expensive freeing so that eligible objects can be reclaimed by a background thread.</p>
<p>The lab verifies that a key is absent immediately after UNLINK succeeds. It does not infer that every byte has returned to the operating system. The Redis 8.2.2 implementation chooses asynchronous freeing according to estimated effort; small objects may be freed directly. Sending a huge list of key names in one command also still requires processing those names.</p>
<p>Moving work to a background thread changes who waits for it. It does not make the CPU work disappear or provide an unlimited reclamation queue. Observe pending lazy-free objects and memory pressure, and keep deletion batches bounded. Otherwise, rapid replacement can allocate new data while older objects are still awaiting reclamation.</p>
<p>Revisit the application’s object boundary if large deletes, huge reads and encoding transitions all centre on the same key. Splitting it may help, but creates more keys, more metadata and possibly more coordination. Use the access pattern and correctness requirement to choose a tradeoff, then measure it.</p>

<h2>Measuring a representative workload</h2>
<p>Begin with an inventory of key families: their counts, value-byte distributions, aggregate sizes, encodings, lifetimes and access rates. Sample representative and unusually large entries. Use MEMORY USAGE carefully; inspecting every element of every large key can itself become expensive maintenance.</p>
<p>Then measure the complete cache call in the application alongside server evidence. SLOWLOG records execution time rather than network transfer. Our lab temporarily logs every command only on its disposable instance to confirm that HGET and HGETALL appear. The individual microsecond observations are not performance promises or a meaningful benchmark comparison.</p>
<p>A useful load experiment preserves the real command mix, value sizes, key popularity, connection and pipeline behaviour, and arrival pattern. Include cold loading, replacements, evictions and configured background operations. Record latency distributions, throughput, errors, source load and memory over time. A high hit rate and good average latency can coexist with an unacceptable tail.</p>
<p>Change one mechanism at a time. Reducing payload size, choosing a smaller projection, bounding maintenance or changing an encoding threshold solve different problems. Repeat the relevant workload and verify both its intended improvement and its costs elsewhere. Capacity is a property of the workload and operating conditions, not a single benchmark number attached to Redis.</p>
<p>The <a href="/learn/databases/caching/examples/redis-memory-lab.zip" download>downloadable memory lab</a> reproduces the allocation and encoding observations, cursor behaviour, command results and UNLINK check. It uses Node 24+, node-redis 6.3.0 and Redis 8.2.2, starting a private Unix-socket process and removing it afterwards. Its README explains the build and version requirements. Exact bytes and timings may differ on your platform.</p>
<p>We can now distinguish retained data, execution work and temporary operational memory. Next, <a href="/learn/databases/caching/replication-and-failover/">Redis replication, persistence and failover</a> examines which copies survive failures, what an acknowledged write establishes, and how recovery changes the cache’s behaviour.</p>

